From: HavenOS <havenos@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/bridge: dw-hdmi: restore the CEC physical address on HPD connect

dw_hdmi_irq() invalidates the CEC physical address when the sink goes away,
but nothing restores it when the sink comes back: the address is only written
from dw_hdmi_connector_get_modes(), which runs when userspace re-probes the
connector after the hotplug uevent.

A DRM master that ignores that uevent - Kodi running on GBM, for instance -
therefore leaves the adapter permanently unaddressed: the TV remote stops
working even though the link, the EDID and the video are all fine, and only a
full restart of the application (which triggers a new modeset) brings CEC
back.

Push the address the last EDID gave us back into the notifier on HPD connect.
If the sink moved to another input the value is stale until the next modeset,
which is no worse than the current permanent invalid state.
---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
index ee88c0e..9a2afdb 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-hdmi.c
@@ -3136,6 +3136,29 @@ static irqreturn_t dw_hdmi_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
 			mutex_lock(&hdmi->cec_notifier_mutex);
 			cec_notifier_phys_addr_invalidate(hdmi->cec_notifier);
 			mutex_unlock(&hdmi->cec_notifier_mutex);
+		} else if (phy_stat & HDMI_PHY_HPD) {
+			u16 pa = hdmi->connector.display_info.source_physical_address;
+
+			/*
+			 * The branch above is the only CEC update this handler
+			 * performs; nothing restores the address on reconnect,
+			 * because it is written from get_modes(), which only
+			 * runs when userspace re-probes the connector after the
+			 * hotplug uevent. A DRM master that ignores the uevent
+			 * (Kodi on GBM) then leaves the adapter unaddressed and
+			 * the TV remote dead until the application restarts.
+			 *
+			 * Reuse the address from the last EDID. Zero means the
+			 * connector was never probed, so there is nothing to
+			 * restore; CEC_PHYS_ADDR_INVALID would only repeat the
+			 * invalidation.
+			 */
+			if (pa && pa != CEC_PHYS_ADDR_INVALID) {
+				mutex_lock(&hdmi->cec_notifier_mutex);
+				cec_notifier_set_phys_addr(hdmi->cec_notifier,
+							   pa);
+				mutex_unlock(&hdmi->cec_notifier_mutex);
+			}
 		}
 
 		if (phy_stat & HDMI_PHY_HPD)
